# CI and configuration

## The workflows

- `.github/workflows/test.yaml` (Test) runs the root scripts in the same order as `bun run check`:
  install → `fmt:check` → `lint` → `typecheck` → package tests → `test:clients` → `test:pack`.
  A new check is added to the root `package.json` scripts **and** to this workflow, so local and
  CI cannot disagree about what "clean" means.
- **Actions are pinned to a commit digest with the tag kept as a comment**:
  `uses: actions/checkout@fbc6f399… # v5.1.0`. A floating tag is a mutable dependency with
  access to the job — never introduce one. Resolve a tag with
  `git ls-remote https://github.com/<owner>/<repo> 'refs/tags/<tag>^{}'`.
- `permissions` stays minimal (`contents: read`); checkout uses `persist-credentials: false`.
- Bun comes from `packageManager` in the root `package.json` (`bun-version-file`); change that
  field to move Bun, not the workflow.
- `.github/workflows/release.yaml` runs after Test succeeds on a push to `main`; see Releasing.
- actionlint (`scripts/actionlint.ts`) and zizmor (`scripts/zizmor.ts`, the workflow security
  audit) download a pinned release and verify its sha256. Bumping one means changing `VERSION`
  and **every checksum** together: actionlint's from the release's `*_checksums.txt`, zizmor's
  from each asset's `digest` in the GitHub releases API.
- A finding is excused next to the thing it is about, with the reason: an actionlint message in
  `.github/actionlint.yaml` scoped to one file, a zizmor audit with an inline
  `# zizmor: ignore[audit]` comment. Never by dropping the check or the setting.
- Root scripts reach a workspace with `bun -F <package> <script>` (`asphodelos`,
  `@asphodelos/test`), never `cd <dir> && …`. Arguments pass through (`bun -F asphodelos test
  --coverage`) and so does a failing exit code.

## Editing the lint configuration

`packages/asphodelos/oxlint.config.ts` is the standard this repository holds itself to, and its
comments explain every choice. `test/oxlint.config.ts` imports it rather than copying it.

- Adding a rule: place it in the section it belongs to and, if the reason is not obvious from
  its name, write the reason above it. Then run `bun run lint` and fix what it reports in the
  code — the rule is not finished until the repository passes it.
- A rule that does not fit this codebase is **left out of the list**, with a comment where it
  would have gone saying why (as `new-cap` is). It is never set to `'off'` in `rules`.
- `'off'` appears only in `overrides`, scoped to the files it describes, with the reason.
- `categories` (correctness / suspicious / perf) are errors, so rules in those categories are not
  restated in `rules`. Only pedantic / style / restriction / nursery rules are listed.
- `plugins` replaces oxlint's defaults, so the defaults are restated there — do not drop one
  while adding another.
- A new `src/` directory gets its `no-restricted-imports` override and a line in the layer map.

## The oxlint plugin

`packages/asphodelos/lint/custom.js` holds the conventions a glob cannot express (the Effect
program shape, function declarations, `no-let`, predicate and type naming). It is an oxlint JS
plugin: untyped ESTree in, a default export out. When adding a rule there:

- Report a message that tells the author what to write instead, in the voice of the existing
  rules — a sentence, not a rule name.
- Exempt `*.test.ts` when the rule describes the shape of `src` rather than a real hazard.
- Add it to the header comment in `custom.js`, to `rules` in `oxlint.config.ts`, and to
  `lint/custom.test.ts` (the registered-rules list and accept / reject cases).

## Formatting and the other linters

- Formatting options live **only** in the root `oxfmt.config.ts`. The same style is duplicated
  as `defaultConfig` in `src/format/index.ts`, because generated code is formatted at runtime;
  change both together.
- `cspell.config.yaml`, `.textlintrc.yml` and `.markdownlint-cli2.jsonc` explain themselves in
  comments; the docs rule says how to live with them.

## Dependencies

- `bun.lock` is written by `bun install`. Never edit it by hand, and never delete it to "fix" an
  install. CI installs with `--frozen-lockfile`.
- `effect`, `@effect/platform-node` and `@effect/platform-node-shared` are pinned exactly and
  move together. A dependency that pulls in another `@effect/*` package with a caret range gets
  that package pinned too: users install with npm, not with bun.lock, and two copies of `effect`
  break the CLI. `bun run test:pack` fails when more than one `effect` is installed.
- The package's runtime `dependencies` are what every user installs. Adding one is a design
  decision — ask first. Tooling goes in `devDependencies` of the workspace that uses it.
- The repository-wide linters are root `devDependencies`, pinned exactly.

## The published package

- `exports` carries three conditions per subpath: `@asphodelos/source` (the `.ts` source, used
  only by the test workspace through `customConditions` in `test/tsconfig.base.json`, so it
  typechecks without a build), then `types` (`dist/*.d.mts`) and `import` (`dist/*.mjs`) for
  users. Keep the three in step when adding a subpath, and add the entry to `tsdown.config.ts`.
- `files: ["dist"]`, plus the `README.md` and `LICENSE` npm always includes. Both sit in
  `packages/asphodelos` as real files because npm leaves symlinks out: the root `README.md` is a
  symlink to the package's, and the root `LICENSE` is a copy kept identical (for GitHub's license
  detection). `test:pack` fails when the two LICENSE files differ.
- There are no lifecycle scripts: `npm publish` does not build, so the release workflow's publish
  job runs `bun -F asphodelos build` right before it.
- `bun run test:pack` is the proof the tarball works for a user: it checks the file list,
  installs it with npm, requires one `effect`, typechecks both entry points, runs the CLI and the
  Vite plugin.

## Releasing

Releases are CI's job, and the `version` field of `packages/asphodelos/package.json` drives them.
Mirroring hono-takibi, `release.yaml` runs after Test passes on a push to `main` and, from the
exact commit Test ran on:

1. **check** — reads the version and asks npm whether it is published;
2. **tag** — creates `v<version>` on that commit through the REST API, or verifies it is already
   there. A version published by hand without a tag is tagged only when npm's recorded `gitHead`
   is this commit; anything else stops with an error that says what to do;
3. **publish** — only when npm does not have the version: `bun -F asphodelos build`, then
   `npm publish` with npm trusted publishing (OIDC, `id-token: write`, environment `release`), so
   no npm token is stored. It is npm rather than `bun publish` because `bun publish` can only
   authenticate with a stored token;
4. **release** — creates the GitHub release for the tag with generated notes.

So the tag, the npm tarball and the GitHub release always describe the same commit, and every job
is safe to re-run. Rules that follow:

- **To release, bump `version` in its own commit whose subject is the version (`0.1.0`), with
  `bun run check` green, and push to `main`.** Nothing else publishes.
- **Never `npm publish` or `bun publish` by hand, and never create or move a `v*` tag by hand.**
  The root is `private` and has a `version` only so npm refuses a root publish with `EPRIVATE`.
- Never reuse or roll back a published version; to fix a release, bump again.
- If publish fails (an npm outage, a missing trusted publisher), fix the cause and re-run the
  failed jobs; the tag already points at the right commit.
- The npm side is configured once, on https://www.npmjs.com/package/asphodelos/access: a trusted
  publisher for this repository, workflow `release.yaml`, environment `release`.
